# Public Constants Contract

`packages/sdk/index.ts` re-exports plain constants alongside RPC methods —
`ModelType`, `MODEL_TYPES`, `PLUGIN_LLM` and friends, `SUPPORTED_AUDIO_FORMATS`,
`TOOLS_MODE`, `VERBOSITY`. Non-JS SDKs (Python, ...) are generated from
`packages/sdk/contract/{schema.json,manifest.json,models.json}` — they never
read `index.ts` directly. A constant that isn't in `schema.json`'s `$defs`
is invisible to every other language, no matter how prominently it's
exported from JS.

`schema.json` is built purely from Zod schemas via `z.toJSONSchema`. A
hand-authored `export const FOO = {...}` with no Zod schema behind it — which
is what `ModelType`, `PLUGIN_*`, and `SUPPORTED_AUDIO_FORMATS` all were
before this rule existed — has no path into the contract at all. It won't
cause a build failure; it just silently never reaches Python (or the next
language).

## The rule

When you add a new constant to `index.ts` that other-language consumers
should get as a typed, named value, you MUST:

1. Express it as a `z.enum(...)` — works for both string and number values,
   and for a bare array of strings via identity mapping (`z.enum(['a','b'])`
   gives `.enum = {a:'a', b:'b'}`).
2. Add it to `constantsRegistry` in `packages/sdk/schemas/constants-
   registry.ts`.
3. Run `bun run contract:export` and commit the resulting `contract/
   schema.json` diff — a new `constants.<Name>` entry in `$defs`.

`build-contract.ts` merges every `constantsRegistry` entry into the same
`$defs` object as every request/response type, via the same
`toWireJsonSchema` call, tagged with an `x-enum-varnames` array (`Object.
keys(schema.enum)`) — a datamodel-code-generator extension that preserves
the registered key names through codegen. Plain JSON Schema `enum:` only
carries *values*; without `x-enum-varnames`, a constant whose keys aren't
mechanically derivable from their values (`PluginId.LLM` vs.
`'@qvac/sdk/llamacpp-completion/plugin'`, `Verbosity.ERROR` vs. `0`) would
silently lose its name and fall back to an ugly auto-slugged one.

```ts
// schemas/constants-registry.ts
export const constantsRegistry = {
  ModelType: z.enum(ModelType),
  // new: MyNewThing: z.enum(MyNewThing),
} as const
```

**If the field that carries this same value in a wire request/response
isn't already a `z.enum(...)`** (e.g. it's a `z.union([z.literal(...), ...])`
of literals), convert it to `z.enum(...)` too, or its own value will remain
untitled in `schema.json` and its name will collide with your registry
entry once both exist as top-level constants of the same name (this bit us
once with `Verbosity` — `llamacpp-config.ts`'s `verbositySchema` was a
`z.union` of literals, invisible to our own titling, and the generator's own
fallback naming for that untitled field collided with the new top-level
`Verbosity` def).

**Scoped to name→value maps only.** A standalone scalar constant (e.g.
`VLA_DEFAULT_IMAGE_SIZE = 512`) has no natural JSON Schema representation as
a plain value — a `const`-only schema generates an awkward `RootModel
[Literal[...]]` wrapper class in Python, not a usable constant — so this
registry doesn't cover scalars. There's no automated path for those yet.

Don't register a constant that's already fully described by a wire schema's
own inline `z.enum`/`z.literal` field type with no separate named-constant
object behind it — that's already in `schema.json` and doesn't need a
second, redundant top-level entry.

See `packages/sdk/contract/README.md` for the exported shape, and
`.cursor/rules/sdk/sdk-pod-packages.mdc` for what counts as an SDK pod
package this applies to.
